    <dependencies>
        <!--
        App dependency: openregister. Recorded as prose, not as an element. The
        App Store schema (info.xsd) has no <app> child under <dependencies>, so
        shipping one fails App Store validation; Nextcloud's DependencyAnalyzer
        does not read it either (it handles only php, database, command, lib,
        os, architecture and the server version), so the element never enforced
        anything at install time. src/manifest.json is where the requirement is
        declared machine-readably.
        -->
        <php min-version="8.3" min-int-size="64"/>
        <database min-version="10">pgsql</database>
        <database>sqlite</database>
        <database min-version="8.0">mysql</database>
        <!--
        min-version is 32, NOT 28. Two independent reasons, both current:

        1. FLEET POLICY. The fleet floor is Nextcloud 32, which is the version
           that guarantees the PHP 8.3 baseline this app already declares below.
           Declaring <php min-version="8.3"/> while advertising NC 28 promises a
           combination we do not build, test or support.

        2. THE openregister APP DEPENDENCY NOTED ABOVE. An app's min-version
           must be >= the max of every app dependency's own floor
           (openconnector#1172/#1173). Every Larpinq entity — characters,
           items, skills, effects, events — is an OpenRegister object, so where
           openregister cannot install this app is non-functional, not merely
           degraded. When openregister's floor was briefly 32 (its
           lib/ContextChat/ContentProvider.php implements
           OCP\ContextChat\IContentProvider, absent from core before NC 32), an
           NC 31 CI leg failed with "App 'Open Register' cannot be installed
           because it is not compatible with this version of the server" — and
           because the shared workflow emits that only as a ##[warning], the run
           carried on and died ~70s later on missing tables, which reads like a
           migration fault and sends you to entirely the wrong file.

           openregister's floor is 28 again at the time of writing
           (openregister#2380, after openregister#2372 removed the eager
           ContextChat reference), so reason 2 is not what forces 32 TODAY —
           reason 1 is. Do not "correct" this back down to match openregister's
           current floor: min-version is a >= bound on dependencies, not an
           equality, and this app has its own PHP floor to honour.

        CI matches this: nextcloud-test-refs in .github/workflows/code-quality.yml
        is ["stable32"] only, so no leg tests a version below the declared floor.
        Check that list before changing this value — PR #282/#284 ping-ponged
        because a 32 floor makes `occ app:enable` refuse on a stable31 leg, and
        the e2e seed then fails with "is not installed or enabled".
        -->
        <nextcloud min-version="32" max-version="34"/>
    </dependencies>
    <!-- Restrict app visibility to the 'larpers' group so that only enrolled GMs and
         players see the navigation entry and can probe endpoints. Admins may add users
         to this group via the Nextcloud admin panel. Closes #216.

         THE GROUP ID 'larpers' IS FROZEN AND DID NOT MOVE WITH THE APP ID.
         A Nextcloud group id is data this app does not own: the group row and its
         memberships live in oc_groups / oc_group_user, provisioned per instance.
         Renaming the id here would not rename that group — it would name a group
         that does not exist, so the restriction would apply to an EMPTY group and
         every enrolled player would silently lose the app. Nothing errors.

         DUAL-GROUP REQUIREMENT: Game Masters need to be in BOTH the built-in 'admin'
         Nextcloud group AND the 'larpers' group. The 'larpers' group controls app
         visibility; admin privileges gate GM-only endpoints (e.g. downloadPdf).
         Regular players only need the 'larpers' group.

         The restriction is configured per instance, not declared here. The App
         Store schema (info.xsd) has no <groups> element, and Nextcloud does not
         read one either: OC_App::listAllApps() overwrites the parsed value with
         the app's `enabled` appconfig entry on every read, so a declaration in
         this file restricted nothing. Use the standard app-level group
         restriction instead: `occ app:enable larpinq` with the groups flag
         (e.g. `groups larpers`), or the group selector on the app's entry in
         the Apps admin page. (XML comments must not contain a double hyphen,
         so the flag is not written with its leading dashes here.) -->

	<!-- THE TWO Migrate* STEPS CARRY THE `larpingapp` -> `larpinq` APP-ID RENAME
	     AND MUST RUN FIRST IN BOTH BLOCKS.

	     There is no in-place app-id upgrade in Nextcloud. Renaming <id> does not
	     rename anything — it makes this app ask for its data under a name nothing
	     answers to, because the new id is simply a different app. Nextcloud
	     namespaces `oc_appconfig` AND `oc_preferences` by app id, so both stores
	     are cut off by the rename, and copying one does nothing for the other.

	     ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING. MigrateAppConfigKeys MUST precede
	     InitializeRegister: InitializeRegister (via SettingsService) writes the
	     imported register/schema bookkeeping keys itself, so if it ran first
	     those keys would already exist under `larpinq` and the never-overwrite
	     guard in the migration would skip them as "already present", stranding
	     the old values under `larpingapp` forever.

	     REGISTERED UNDER <install> AS WELL AS <post-migration>. <install> is the
	     path a real deployment takes, because Nextcloud discovers `larpinq` as a
	     brand-new app rather than as an upgrade of `larpingapp`. That is also
	     why both steps log-and-continue instead of throwing: a repair step that
	     throws under <install> does not merely fail an upgrade — the app never
	     enables, and every route goes with it. -->
	<repair-steps>
		<post-migration>
			<!-- Copy every stored IAppConfig value from the `larpingapp` namespace. -->
			<step>OCA\Larpinq\Repair\MigrateAppConfigKeys</step>
			<!-- Copy every per-user preference from the `larpingapp` app id. -->
			<step>OCA\Larpinq\Repair\MigrateUserPreferences</step>
			<!-- Third leg of the rename, on the OpenRegister register ROW. The
			     register slug moves too (`larpingapp` -> `larpinq`), together
			     with `x-openregister.app`, which for a `type: application`
			     configuration IS a register slug rather than an attribution
			     label. MUST run before InitializeRegister: OpenRegister resolves
			     a register by slug and its not-found branch CREATES a second,
			     empty register rather than failing, so an import that ran first
			     would fork the register and leave every stored object behind on
			     the old row, silently. Moves no data — an object is bound to its
			     register by NUMERIC id. Idempotent and non-destructive. -->
			<step>OCA\Larpinq\Repair\MigrateRegisterSlug</step>
			<!-- SIBLING OF MigrateRegisterSlug, AND NEITHER COVERS THE OTHER.
			     OpenRegister resolves the two halves of a configuration by
			     DIFFERENT keys: a REGISTER by slug alone
			     (RegisterMapper::find), a SCHEMA by the PAIR
			     (SchemaMapper::findByApplicationAndSlug(slug, application)).
			     This app now passes `appId: Application::APP_ID` = `larpinq`,
			     while every schema it already owns still carries
			     `application = larpingapp` — so the pair matches nothing, and
			     ImportHandler's not-found branch is not an error path, it is
			     the "create a new one" path. The import below would build a
			     SECOND, EMPTY schema set under the new application id while
			     every stored object stayed bound to the old rows. Nothing
			     errors; the app renders empty collections.

			     Measured on a live install: 2 schemas still under `larpingapp`,
			     and BOTH already have a twin under `larpinq` — the fork has
			     already happened here. This step therefore REFUSES both and
			     logs them, because re-pointing would leave two rows sharing
			     (application, slug) and findByApplicationAndSlug() caps at one
			     row: one would silently win every lookup and the other's
			     objects would become unreachable. Choosing between them is a
			     decision about data, not a migration.

			     Never deletes a schema, and never throws — it runs under
			     <install>, where an escaping exception aborts the install and
			     the app never enables. -->
			<step>OCA\Larpinq\Repair\MigrateSchemaApplicationId</step>
			<step>OCA\Larpinq\Repair\InitializeRegister</step>
		</post-migration>
		<install>
			<step>OCA\Larpinq\Repair\MigrateAppConfigKeys</step>
			<step>OCA\Larpinq\Repair\MigrateUserPreferences</step>
			<!-- Third leg of the rename, on the OpenRegister register ROW. The
			     register slug moves too (`larpingapp` -> `larpinq`), together
			     with `x-openregister.app`, which for a `type: application`
			     configuration IS a register slug rather than an attribution
			     label. MUST run before InitializeRegister: OpenRegister resolves
			     a register by slug and its not-found branch CREATES a second,
			     empty register rather than failing, so an import that ran first
			     would fork the register and leave every stored object behind on
			     the old row, silently. Moves no data — an object is bound to its
			     register by NUMERIC id. Idempotent and non-destructive. -->
			<step>OCA\Larpinq\Repair\MigrateRegisterSlug</step>
			<!-- SIBLING OF MigrateRegisterSlug, AND NEITHER COVERS THE OTHER.
			     OpenRegister resolves the two halves of a configuration by
			     DIFFERENT keys: a REGISTER by slug alone
			     (RegisterMapper::find), a SCHEMA by the PAIR
			     (SchemaMapper::findByApplicationAndSlug(slug, application)).
			     This app now passes `appId: Application::APP_ID` = `larpinq`,
			     while every schema it already owns still carries
			     `application = larpingapp` — so the pair matches nothing, and
			     ImportHandler's not-found branch is not an error path, it is
			     the "create a new one" path. The import below would build a
			     SECOND, EMPTY schema set under the new application id while
			     every stored object stayed bound to the old rows. Nothing
			     errors; the app renders empty collections.

			     Measured on a live install: 2 schemas still under `larpingapp`,
			     and BOTH already have a twin under `larpinq` — the fork has
			     already happened here. This step therefore REFUSES both and
			     logs them, because re-pointing would leave two rows sharing
			     (application, slug) and findByApplicationAndSlug() caps at one
			     row: one would silently win every lookup and the other's
			     objects would become unreachable. Choosing between them is a
			     decision about data, not a migration.

			     Never deletes a schema, and never throws — it runs under
			     <install>, where an escaping exception aborts the install and
			     the app never enables. -->
			<step>OCA\Larpinq\Repair\MigrateSchemaApplicationId</step>
			<step>OCA\Larpinq\Repair\InitializeRegister</step>
		</install>
	</repair-steps>

